WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:06.450
Film Courage: Corey, from your time in film
school and also having writing instructors

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:12.740
speak with you when you were a college student,
did you feel embraced or discouraged?

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:16.449
Something like what I heard in an old Ray
Bradbury interview where he advised a lot

00:00:16.449 --> 00:00:20.880
of people not to take writing classes because
he felt they [the students] were always discouraged

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:22.349
in the class.

00:00:22.349 --> 00:00:24.100
How did you feel as a student?

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:26.779
Corey Mandell: As a student, really encouraged.

00:00:26.779 --> 00:00:27.779
Film Courage: Did you?

00:00:27.779 --> 00:00:28.779
Okay.

00:00:28.779 --> 00:00:32.750
Corey Mandell: But that is actually why I
would…have people think twice before taking

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:37.190
those classes because there is fixed mindset
encouragement and there is growth mindset

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:39.969
encouragement.

00:00:39.969 --> 00:00:41.940
The very first teacher that I…let me step
back.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:47.170
A lot of teachers that I know have a philosophy
that the more you write, the better you will

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:48.170
become.

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:50.000
And so their job is to encourage writers.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.270
And you are going to point out things they
are not doing right.

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:58.499
You are going to criticize but you always
couch that with three compliments, you know?

00:00:58.499 --> 00:00:59.879
Like the sugar with the medicine.

00:00:59.879 --> 00:01:00.879
Film Courage: [Laughs] Sure.

00:01:00.879 --> 00:01:03.059
Corey Mandell: Ultimately, at the end of the
day, your job is to make these people feel

00:01:03.059 --> 00:01:06.090
like they can do this, that they have what
it takes.

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:09.260
And that will keep them writing and the more
they write, they better they will become.

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:11.910
And I don’t do that as a teacher for a variety
of reasons.

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:16.400
First (as I said) I think most writers when
they first start writing, they see significant

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:17.400
improvement.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:20.440
Because when we first start writing, we make
lots of really dumb mistakes that we don’t

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:21.440
know.

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:22.510
We don’t know what we don’t know.

00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:27.510
So if you are somewhat open to feedback and
criticism, you’re trying to get better and

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:32.410
you have really smart (or reasonably smart
people) in your life and giving you feedback,

00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:36.750
I do think people start to make a really good
improvement then they’ll hit a plateau that

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:38.560
they will stay on forever.

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:44.690
And that plateau is the more they write, the
higher the pile of some early flawed scripts

00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:45.690
are.

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:48.390
Because if they have intuitive weaknesses
or conceptual weaknesses, you can keep writing

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:52.420
as much as you want and you’re going to
keep making those mistakes.

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:57.000
I know writers who have been at it for 10
or 12 years and what they’re writing today

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.720
isn’t really better than what they’re
writing 8 years ago.

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:04.060
So I don’t believe…I believe the more
you write, the better you become but without

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:11.009
the right training and tools (for most people)
is kind of a Santa Claus/Tooth Fairy fantasy.

00:02:11.009 --> 00:02:15.350
Further, I do think it is important to have
someone in your life or people in your life

00:02:15.350 --> 00:02:19.481
who believe in you and love you and support
you and when you’re feeling down they are

00:02:19.481 --> 00:02:20.481
like “No.

00:02:20.481 --> 00:02:21.481
You can do this!”

00:02:21.481 --> 00:02:25.920
And they can be the wind in your sails when
the wind goes away and you just sort of lose

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:27.340
confidence and you lose your way.

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:29.860
I think that’s absolutely essential.

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:31.980
I would never do that as a teacher.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:35.340
I don’t think someone should pay for that
and also I don’t want someone to become

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:36.590
dependent on me.

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:41.980
I want them to find people in their lives
and ultimately themselves to be able to have

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:46.200
that confidence and love.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:51.650
Further, I think the fixed mindset confidence
is always pointing out what you’re doing

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:53.700
right and complimenting “You’re so good.

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:54.700
You’re so good.

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:55.700
You have what it takes.”

00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:57.110
That’s so fixed mindset.

00:02:57.110 --> 00:03:00.600
The growth mindset is there are tools that
you can learn.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:02.290
And let me teach you a tool.

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:05.030
So what is your biggest weakness as a writer?

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:09.350
Let’s spend four weeks doing nothing but
training on that tool.

00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:12.650
And then, we’re going to have you write
a scene or a sequence and you’re going to

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:16.720
see (or you give it to other people and they’re
going to see) “Oh my gosh.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:18.940
You’re suddenly better at this thing!”

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:20.310
and then you’re like “Oh?!”

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:22.610
That is the confidence that I want to give
someone.

00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:23.610
I had a weakness.

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:25.860
I identified (or it was identified for me).

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:29.660
I had a specific training regiment and I came
out the other end stronger at that.

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:33.000
And maybe you’re not as strong as you need
to be yet but you can keep training and you

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:36.990
can keep training and you can realize that
I can get to the point where I’m as good

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:37.990
as I need to be.

00:03:37.990 --> 00:03:41.190
Okay, now maybe I’ve got six things that
you need to do that on and so maybe it’s

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:43.490
going to take 6 months or a year.

00:03:43.490 --> 00:03:45.270
That’s growth mindset confidence.

00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:48.430
That’s what I want to instill in people.

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:53.650
So my experience for the most part in film
school was very gentle.

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:55.959
Everyone came in and told us “You can make
this.

00:03:55.959 --> 00:03:56.959
You can do this.

00:03:56.959 --> 00:03:58.819
You’re amazing.”

00:03:58.819 --> 00:04:01.160
And in my class there were 12 of us.

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:06.240
Three of us went on to have careers and 9
didn’t.

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:12.010
And one thing that the three of us had in
common, we weren’t smart than…I certainly

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:15.140
wasn’t smart than some of the others or
a lot of the others.

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:16.340
We didn’t work harder.

00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:18.310
Honestly, I didn’t work that hard in film
school.

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:23.759
I’m not proud of that but I had a lot of
procrastination issues.

00:04:23.759 --> 00:04:27.050
What we did have in common was that we had
a manager (not the same manager).

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:32.879
But we all had a manager who in their own
way said “You are not as good as you think

00:04:32.879 --> 00:04:33.879
you are.

00:04:33.879 --> 00:04:37.509
And you need to get better in certain skill
sets and I can help you do that.”

00:04:37.509 --> 00:04:44.050
And in my case, it was a year of working with
this manager turning weaknesses that I didn’t

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:46.990
know I had (or I did know I had) into strengths.

00:04:46.990 --> 00:04:50.830
That’s a big reason why I teach because
I had access to that.

00:04:50.830 --> 00:04:55.159
If I didn’t, I would have been like the
9 who didn’t.

00:04:55.159 --> 00:04:59.629
I had certain things that I was really inherently
good at and certain things I was inherently

00:04:59.629 --> 00:05:04.729
really bad at and I had things I didn’t
know I was bad at.

00:05:04.729 --> 00:05:08.389
And no one in film school was really helping
me with that.

00:05:08.389 --> 00:05:12.389
It was the manager that did that work.

00:05:12.389 --> 00:05:17.790
So I think yeah…classes where they just
kind of tear you down and discourage you should

00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:21.419
be avoided at all costs.

00:05:21.419 --> 00:05:25.339
I know people who have troubles with their
writing and they don’t feel confident and

00:05:25.339 --> 00:05:29.630
they find a teacher who gives them confidence
and support and they love that teacher and

00:05:29.630 --> 00:05:30.759
they become addicted to that teacher.

00:05:30.759 --> 00:05:35.180
And it’s a good business model for the teacher
because they’ll keep taking classes because

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:38.300
they’re taking classes to feel that feeling.

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:40.330
But that’s not solving the problem.

00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:44.340
If I can make you feel confident or good about
your writing or special or I can make you

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:47.419
feel like you can get there, that doesn't
mean anything.

00:05:47.419 --> 00:05:50.199
What matters is when you can feel that way.

00:05:50.199 --> 00:05:54.770
And the way you’ll feel that way (in my
opinion) is through proving to yourself that

00:05:54.770 --> 00:05:57.559
you’re making progress and confidence through
your training.

00:05:57.559 --> 00:06:03.229
Film Courage: Sound it sounds like this sort
of military style, you know, boot camp, breaking

00:06:03.229 --> 00:06:05.169
you down is going to harm someone.

00:06:05.169 --> 00:06:09.330
But at the same time too much hand-holding
and babying and “Oh, you’re one of our

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:10.870
prized student!”

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:12.569
that sounds like it’s equally damaging?

00:06:12.569 --> 00:06:13.629
Like you’re the golden child.

00:06:13.629 --> 00:06:16.360
Corey Mandell: It’s highly-addicting and
you’re going to feel good but that’s like

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:17.360
junk food.

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:18.879
It tastes good but it’s not nutritious.

00:06:18.879 --> 00:06:20.069
It’s not making you healthier.

00:06:20.069 --> 00:06:25.710
Film Courage: So with this manager, do you
think you were more open hearing than some

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:26.710
of the other people.

00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:30.990
I mean if you feel that you were on the same
level, was there a difference that you were

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:31.990
maybe more open?

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:36.020
Corey Mandell: Right…That’s a great question
and as I said earlier, I was such an incredibly

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:37.520
fixed mindset person.

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:45.779
So I was absolutely not open to hearing and
I want to share this because I try to share

00:06:45.779 --> 00:06:49.770
this with as many writers as I can because
I think it’s a really important story and

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:52.339
there is something here that they can do which
I would highly-recommend.

00:06:52.339 --> 00:06:55.669
So what happened is I wrote this script and…

